ical Hypotheses\n1. **Hypothesis 1 (High-Precision Parity at INT8)**:\n   - At INT8, activation outliers do not cause catastrophic clipping noise. Vanilla RTN, Vanilla GPTQ, QuaRot-RTN, and QuaRot-GPTQ will all maintain within $< 0.1$ PPL and $< 0.5\\%$ accuracy degradation relative to FP16.\n2. **Hypothesis 2 (Outlier Emergence at INT6)**:\n   - At INT6, unrotated RTN will begin to degrade ($\\Delta \\text{PPL} > 0.5$) due to kurtosis in activation channels. QuaRot-RTN will successfully eliminate this gap, performing within $0.2\\%$ of FP16 without needing GPTQ.\n3. **Hypothesis 3 (Second-Order Compensation Dependency at INT4)**:\n   - At INT4, unrotated RTN collapses ($\\text{PPL} > 50$ or $\\text{NaN}$).\n   - QuaRot-RTN stabilizes the model ($\\text{PPL} \\approx 6.5 - 7.5$), demonstrating that outlier suppression is sufficient for basic coherence.\n   - QuaRot-GPTQ provides critical second-order weight compensation, reducing the remaining quantization noise and achieving near-lossless zero-shot performance ($< 2.0\\%$ drop from FP16 baseline).\n\n### 6.2 Analytical Breakdowns\n- **Gap Reduction Metric**: Compute how much of the performance deficit $\\text{Gap} = \\text{Score}_{\\text{FP16}} - \\text{Score}_{\\text{Vanilla}}$ is recovered by rotation alone vs. rotation + GPTQ.\n- **Activation Kurtosis Profiling**: Measure the maximum activation kurtosis $\\kappa = \\frac{\\mathbb{E}[(X - \\mu)^4]}{(\\sigma^2)^2}$ before and after applying $Q_{\\text{model}}$ to confirm outlier dispersion across the non-standard dimension $d=3072$.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility, Resource Estimation & Verification Checks\n\n### 7.1 Computational & Hardware Feasibility\n- **Model Footprint**: Phi-3-mini (~3.8B parameters) in FP16 requires ~7.6 GB VRAM; in INT4 weights require ~1.9 GB VRAM.\n- **Hardware Requirement**: A single NVIDIA A100 (40GB/80GB) or RTX 4090 (24GB) GPU is sufficient for both calibration and evaluation.\n- **Runtime Budget**:\n  - Offline rotation + GPTQ calibration (128 seqs): ~15 minutes per INT-level.\n  - Perplexity evaluation (WikiText-2 + C4): ~10 minutes per configuration.\n  - Zero-shot evaluation suite (6 tasks): ~35 minutes per configuration.\n  - Total compute for all 13 experimental configurations: $\\approx 11 - 13$ GPU-hours.\n\n### 7.2 Sanity & Verification Checks\n1. **Orthogonality Preservation**: Verify $\\| Q^T Q - I_d \\|_F < 10^{-6}$ for the Kronecker-factored matrix $Q_{3072} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3072}} (H_3 \\otimes H_{1024})$.\n2. **Mathematical Invariance Check**: In full FP32/FP16 precision, verify that passing activations through rotated weights and online FWHT yields output identical to the unrotated model within floating-point tolerance ($\\| Y_{\\text{rotated}} - Y_{\\text{unrotated}} \\| / \\| Y \\| < 10^{-5}$).\n3. **Calibration Overfitting Check**: Confirm perplexity on held-out C4 does not diverge compared to WikiText-2, ensuring GPTQ Hessian estimates do not overfit to calibration distribution.", "summary": "Delivered full experiment design comparing QuaRot-RTN and QuaRot-GPTQ across INT4, INT6, and INT8 on Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct.", "evidence": "Inspected repository and verified task specifications. Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct possesses hidden dimension 3072 (factored as 3 x 1024) and intermediate dimension 8192 (2^13), which aligns with Kronecker-factored and Sylvester-Hadamard transforms. Experimental design includes 13 balanced configurations across precisions and quantization algorithms with controls and resource profiles.", "assumptions": ["Target model is microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct loaded in Hugging Face Transformers-compatible format.", "Quantization precision is evaluated symmetrically across weights, activations, and KV cache (W{b}A{b}KV{b}) for INT4, INT6, and INT8.", "Non-power-of-two dimensions (3072, 96) are accommodated using Kronecker-factored orthogonal Walsh-Hadamard matrices.", "Evaluation is conducted in zero-shot mode without any task-specific fine-tuning or post-quantiza